  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <div class="lang-switch">
    <a href="#en">English</a>
    <a href="#es">Español</a>
    <a href="#hi">हिन्दी</a>
    <a href="#pt">Português</a>
    <a href="#tr">Türkçe</a>
  </div>
  <div class="effective-date">Effective Date: October 17, 2026</div>

  <h2>1. Information Collection and Use</h2>
  <p>Tether Link Utility is designed as a local system routing utility.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No Account:</strong> The app has no accounts and does not collect your name, phone number, or account details. It collects an email address only if you choose to type one into the optional help form (see section 9).</li>
    <li><strong>No Network Traffic Logging:</strong> We do not monitor, log, inspect, or store the content of your traffic, your browsing history, or the destination addresses passing through your tethering connection, and this traffic is not sent to any server of ours.</li>
    <li><strong>Third-Party Services:</strong> The app includes Google AdMob (free version only), Firebase Analytics, Firebase Crashlytics, and Firebase Remote Config. These services collect device identifiers and usage and crash data, as described in section 3. Help requests you choose to send from the app are stored in Google Firestore and emailed to us through Formspree (section 9).</li>
    <li><strong>Location and Nearby devices:</strong> Android only lets an app start its own Wi-Fi hotspot with the Location permission (Android 8 to 12) or the Nearby devices permission (Android 13 and later). The app asks for it only to start that hotspot. It never reads, stores, or sends your location.</li>
    <li><strong>Camera:</strong> Used only to scan QR codes. Images stay on your device and are not saved or sent.</li>
    <li><strong>iPhone and iPad:</strong> On iPhone and iPad the app does not ask for location; it uses the camera for QR codes and local network access to reach your other device.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Use of Android VpnService and iOS Network Extension</h2>
  <p>Our application uses the Android VpnService API to establish a local SOCKS5 proxy server on your device.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> The VpnService is used strictly to protect local socket connections from being re-routed by the system, allowing the app to forward hotspot traffic through the device's active GSM network connection. It does not connect to any remote VPN server.</li>
    <li><strong>No Remote Server:</strong> Traffic handled by the service goes from your connected devices to your phone over its Wi-Fi hotspot, and then out over your mobile data connection to the sites and services you use. It is not routed through, copied to, or stored on any server operated by us.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On iPhone and iPad, the app uses Apple's Network Extension framework (a packet tunnel) for the same purposes.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sharing from an iPhone:</strong> the tunnel keeps the local SOCKS5 proxy server running while the app is in the background. It does not route this iPhone's own traffic and does not connect to any remote VPN server.</li>
    <li><strong>Connecting to another phone:</strong> the tunnel sends this device's traffic to the proxy on the phone you chose, for example by scanning its QR code. It is not sent to any server of ours.</li>
    <li><strong>Saved hotspot details:</strong> if you save your hotspot's Wi-Fi name and password so the QR code can include them, they are stored only on your device (on iOS, the password is kept in the Keychain) and are never sent to us.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Advertising &amp; Analytics</h2>
  <p>The free version displays interstitial and banner ads served by Google AdMob. AdMob may collect device identifiers and usage data in accordance with Google's Privacy Policy. Where required, the app asks for your consent first with Google's consent message, and you can change your choice at any time with "Privacy options" in the app. Premium subscribers do not see ads.</p>
  <p>We also use Firebase Analytics (Google) to understand basic usage of the app, such as how many people open it and which features are used, so we can find problems and improve it. Firebase Analytics collects an app instance identifier, device information (such as model and OS version), and usage events. We do not link this data to your name, phone number, or email address.</p>
  <p>We use Firebase Crashlytics (Google) to receive crash reports and, when a connection to Google or Apple services keeps failing, a short technical note about which step failed (no personal data). A crash report includes device information, the app's state at the time of the crash, and an installation identifier. We use Firebase Remote Config (Google) to change some app settings without an app update; to do this, the app sends an app instance identifier and basic device information to Firebase.</p>
  <p>These services are provided by Google and handle data according to <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's Privacy Policy</a>. None of them receive the content of the traffic you share through the proxy.</p>
  <p>Google may estimate an approximate location (such as your country or city) from your IP address for these services. The app itself never reads your device's location.</p>

  <h2>4. In-App Purchases</h2>
  <p>Subscriptions are processed securely through Google Play Billing on Android and the App Store on iPhone and iPad. We do not receive or store your payment details. The purchase status (Free or Premium, and when it was last confirmed) is stored encrypted on your device so the app works without a connection, and it is re-checked with the store whenever possible. If the store cannot be reached, a previously confirmed Premium status stays valid for up to 7 days offline.</p>

  <h2>5. Data Security</h2>
  <p>All proxy operations are handled on your device, with no remote server of ours in between. The traffic you share through the proxy goes from your devices to your phone and out over your mobile connection; it is not sent to us. Advertising, analytics, and crash data are handled as described in section 3. Help requests are handled as described in section 9.</p>

  <h2>6. Children's Privacy</h2>
  <p>This application is not directed at children under the age of 13. We do not knowingly collect any information from children.</p>

  <h2>7. Changes to This Privacy Policy</h2>
  <p>We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. Changes are effective when posted.</p>

  <h2>8. Your Choices and Data Deletion</h2>
  <p>The app has no accounts, so we do not hold data tied to your name, phone number or email address, except an email you choose to leave in a help request (section 9). You can still control and remove the data described above:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Advertising ID:</strong> you can reset or delete your advertising ID, or opt out of personalized ads, in your Android settings (usually under Google &gt; Ads, or Privacy &gt; Ads, depending on the device).</li>
    <li><strong>Tracking on iPhone and iPad:</strong> the app asks for permission before using your device's advertising identifier. You can change this at any time in Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Tracking; if you do not allow it, you still see ads, but they are not personalized using that identifier.</li>
    <li><strong>Data on your device:</strong> clearing the app's storage or uninstalling the app removes the settings and identifiers it keeps on your phone. Your phone's system backup may restore a few of them, such as the port and the saved hotspot password, if you reinstall.</li>
    <li><strong>Deletion request:</strong> email <a href="mailto:support@tetherlinkutility.com?subject=Data%20deletion%20request">support@tetherlinkutility.com</a> with the subject "Data deletion request". Because this data is not linked to your name or email, please include your device model and roughly when you used the app so we can try to locate it. We will ask Google to delete the Firebase Analytics and Crashlytics data we can associate with your installation, and we aim to reply within 30 days.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="help-requests">9. Help Requests</h2>
  <p>If you can't connect, you can tell us where you got stuck with the "Can't connect?" form on this website or from the app. Sending one is always your choice.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>From this website:</strong> the form sends what you were trying to connect (your device type), where you got stuck, your message if you add one, your email address if you add one (used only to reply to you), and the page language. It is processed by Formspree (Formspree, Inc., formspree.io) as our service provider, only to deliver it to our support inbox. Like any web service, Formspree also receives technical data such as your IP address to deliver the form and block spam, as described in <a href="https://formspree.io/legal/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Formspree's privacy policy</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>From the app:</strong> a help request sent from inside the Android or iPhone app is stored in our Firebase project (Google Firestore, EU region). It contains your choices (device type, where you got stuck and what went wrong), your message if you add one (up to 500 characters), your email address if you add one (used only to reply to you), a random ID created only for help requests (so we can group requests from the same install), and non-personal app diagnostics: app version, Android or iOS version, device maker and model, app language, whether you were sharing or using another phone's internet, whether sharing was on, connection counts, which guide step you were on, the result of the connection check and of joining the Wi-Fi, and the last app screens you went through before connecting (screen names and time spent, no content; kept only in the app's memory and sent only with your request). Your IP address is not stored, and a request never includes your Wi-Fi name or password, advertising ID (or Apple's IDFA or IDFV), or location. Our server then forwards it to our support inbox by email through Formspree (Formspree, Inc.), our service provider for email delivery. We use it only to give you support, and it is automatically deleted after 90 days.</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> to answer you and to improve the setup guide and the app.</li>
    <li><strong>How long:</strong> we keep help requests for up to 90 days, then delete them.</li>
    <li><strong>Not sold or shared:</strong> we do not sell help requests or share them with anyone beyond the processing described above (Google and Formspree, as our service providers).</li>
    <li><strong>Deletion:</strong> to have a request deleted sooner, email <a href="mailto:support@tetherlinkutility.com?subject=Data%20deletion%20request">support@tetherlinkutility.com</a> and tell us roughly when you sent it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>10. Contact Us</h2>
  <p>If you have any questions about this Privacy Policy, please contact us at: <a href="mailto:support@tetherlinkutility.com">support@tetherlinkutility.com</a></p>
  <p><a href="/help.html">Can't connect? Tell us where you got stuck</a></p>

  <h2>11. Intellectual Property</h2>
  <p>Barnah Adam, Barnah Kız, and all other characters, artwork, and content associated with Tether Link Utility are original works. All rights reserved.</p>
  </div>

